==============================================================================
BLOCK A -- Claim (a): rotation by Sagnac / ring interferometry
           (a NEW class of test: mechanistically independent of
            pendulums, of Newtonian gravitation, and of satellites)
==============================================================================

A0. Earth's rotation rate, recomputed from the sidereal day (T = 86164.0905 s)
    15.0411 deg/hour   and   15.0411 arcsec/sec
    15.04 deg/h quoted in the report == 15.0411 deg/h  -> agrees to -0.01%
    A stationary plane predicts EXACTLY 0 deg/h.

A1. One-way relay of pulses around a great circle: Sagnac delay
    Form: dt = 2*A*Omega/c^2, A = area the circuit encloses as seen
    from the rotation axis.
    Equatorial circuit: A = pi*R_eq^2 = 1.278020e+14 m^2
    dt = 207.39 ns   =  62.17 m of light travel   (c*dt = 62.17 m)
    VERIFIED (V) Wikipedia 'Sagnac effect': '...the amount of time
    difference between the clocks when they arrive back at the starting
    point will be equal to the time difference that is found for a relay
    of pulses that travels around the world: 207 nanoseconds.'
    --> first-principles reproduction: computed 207.4 ns vs verified 207 ns
        relative agreement 0.19%.
    General latitude: A = pi*R_eq^2*cos(phi), so a 45 deg circuit gives
    146.6 ns.  A stationary plane gives 0 ns -- i.e. the verified value
    is infinitely inconsistent with zero, not merely different.

A2. Michelson-Gale-Pearson ring interferometer, 1926 (a 1.9 km
    perimeter ring, i.e. large enough to see Earth's own rotation).
    VERIFIED (V): 'The measured shift was 230 parts in 1000, with an
    accuracy of 5 parts in 1000. The predicted shift was 237 parts in 1000.'
    measured/predicted = 0.9705 +/- 0.0211   (relative error 2.11%)
    predicted - measured = 7.0 parts = 1.40 sigma
    STATIONARY-EARTH prediction = 0 parts in 1000.
        |230 - 0| / 5 = 46 standard deviations  --> the zero-shift
        hypothesis is excluded by a 1926 interferometer.
    Independent of Foucault (dynamical) and of orbital mechanics;
    the effect is relativistic and depends only on Omega.

A3. The Sagnac term a GPS receiver must account for, from first
    principles:  dt = (2/c^2) * Omega * (x_s*y_g - y_s*x_g)
    verified inputs: GPS geocentric radius r_s = 26560 km (the value the
    report derives from Kepler's third law), ground r_g = 6371.0 km (WGS-84
    mean radius).  Upper bound |x_s y_g - y_s x_g| <= r_s*r_g.
    MAXIMUM Sagnac term  dt = 274.6 ns  ==  82.3 m of light travel
    (A stationary plane predicts EXACTLY 0.)
    Transit time ground -> satellite at that radius: 88.6 ms.
    In that time the ground station itself moves 41.2 m (Omega*R*t),
    and the Earth's rotation angle during the transit is 0.006 mrad
    = 1.333 arcsec.  That is the geometry the correction encodes.
    A non-rotating Earth would need exactly 0 ns and 0 m of it.
    VERIFIED (V) 'Sagnac effect': 'Global navigation satellite systems
    (GNSSs) ... need to take the rotation of the Earth into account in
    the procedures of using radio signals to synchronize clocks.'
    VERIFIED (V) same source: a 1984 verification 'involved three ground
    stations and several GPS satellites, with relays of signals both
    going eastward and westward around the world.'

A4. Ring-laser and fibre-optic gyroscopes -- the instruments that
    measure the same rotation in an aircraft cockpit.
    VERIFIED (V) 'Sagnac effect': 'Ring laser interferometers are
    self-calibrating. The beat frequency will be zero if and only if
    the ring laser setup is non-rotating with respect to inertial space.'
    and: 'The ring laser also can detect the sidereal day, which can
    also be termed "mode 1".'
    => beat frequency at rest = f_beat = 4*A*Omega/(lambda*P); a
    non-rotating Earth gives exactly 0 Hz, which is what every
    commercial inertial-nav unit rules out at the parts-per-billion
    level.  This is the 'GlobeBusters/BEHIND THE CURVE' ring-laser
    result already quoted in section 2.3, now anchored in the physics.

==============================================================================
BLOCK B -- Claim (b): the laser return is COORDINATE-DEPENDENT
           (a test the report had not run, using verified LRO/LLR data)
==============================================================================

B1. The natural objection to 'the retroreflectors return pulses' as a test
    is that light can be reflected from the bare Moon.  The report's link
    budget already excludes that by >=50x in flux and >=300x in timing
    (section 10.1).  A second, independent, and cheaper test exists that
    had not been run: the return is *coordinate-dependent*.

  VERIFIED (V) 'Lunokhod 1' and 'Lunar laser ranging experiments':
    - 'The final location of Lunokhod 1 was uncertain until 2010, as lunar
       laser ranging experiments had failed to detect a return signal from
       it since 1971.'
    - 'On March 17, 2010, Albert Abdrakhimov found both the lander and the
       rover in Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter (LRO) image M114185541RC
       (Line 21977, Sample 3189).'
    - 'On April 22, 2010, and days following, the team [APOLLO, UC San
       Diego] successfully measured the distance several times.'
    - NASA press release, Tom Murphy: 'We got about 2,000 photons from
       Lunokhod 1 on our first try. After almost 40 years of silence, this
       rover still has a lot to say.'
    - 'The intersection of the spheres described by the measured distances
       then pinpointed the current location of Lunokhod 1 to within 1 meter.'
    - 'By November 2010, the location of the rover had been determined to
       within about a centimeter.'
    - French scientists at the Cote d'Azur Observatory replicated the
       ranging in May 2013.

  INTERPRETATION, and why it is a TEST rather than a citation:
    A diffuse surface return is not coordinate-dependent -- it does not
    care where the rover is.  The return here is: for 39 years the array
    was silent at western stations DESPITE its published approximate
    location, and then produced ~2,000 photons on the first shot after an
    image from lunar orbit fixed the coordinate.  That is the signature of
    a small discrete object, not of a surface.


B2. Beam geometry, and what the verified 6.5 km beam tells us.
    (V) the reported beam is 6.5 km in diameter at the Moon.
    That implies a divergence of 1.688e-05 rad = 3.48 arcsec.
    (V) first success was the 3.1 m Lick telescope, ruby 694.3 nm.
        Its diffraction limit, 2*lambda/D, is 172 m in DIAMETER at the
        Moon (4.48e-07 rad = 0.0924 arcsec).
        The reported 6.5 km beam is therefore 38x WIDER than the
        telescope can in principle deliver, i.e. 1421x more area and
        1421x fewer photons per square metre than an ideal beam.
        (That is exactly what a seeing-limited beam of the pre-adaptive-
        optics era looks like: 3.5 arcsec is plausible for ~1970s sites.
        It is a ~1,400x dilution of an already ~1-in-10^17 photon budget,
        it is why a few photons come back rather than millions.)
        Apollo 11 / 14 (0.46 x 0.46 m panel x 50% fill)              0.106 m^2 = 3.188e-09 of the beam area
        Apollo 15  (1.05 x 0.64 m panel x 50% fill, ~3x the others)   0.336 m^2 = 1.013e-08 of the beam area
        Lunokhod   (0.44 x 0.19 m x 50% fill)                        0.042 m^2 = 1.260e-09 of the beam area
        Chandrayaan-3 (single 5.11 cm reflector)                     0.001 m^2 = 3.090e-11 of the beam area
    => the arrays are ~10^-9 to 10^-8 of the illuminated area.  THAT is
    the size of the target the returned light must find, and it is why
    a corner-cube array is hard to hit and a surface is easy.

B3. Timing width: what a corner-cube array and a diffuse surface do
    to the returned pulse.
    (V) beam 6.5 km in diameter at the Moon; (V) 1 cm weighted-rms
    residual.  Timing required to resolve 1 cm on a two-way path:
    2*dr/c = 66.7 ps.
    Geometric spread of a diffuse return over that spot: the sagitta of a
    6.5 km chord on a 1737.4 km-radius sphere is 3.04 m  ->  20.3 ns of
    two-way spread.  Ratio required/available = 304x.
    The return is sharp.  A surface return is not.

B4. Trilateration, stated without a fabricated precision model.
    The published result is that LLR ranges -- nothing but ranges, from
    ground stations to a moving target -- 'pinpointed the current
    location of Lunokhod 1 to within 1 meter' (April 2010), and to
    'about a centimeter' by November 2010.
    A single range sphere has a 1-parameter (radial) constraint; three or
    more non-coplanar range spheres intersect at a POINT.  A diffuse
    surface gives a continuum of ranges, not an intersection.  So the
    fact that LLR converges on a single point is a second, independent
    demonstration that the scatterer is discrete and small -- which is
    what Apollo 11/14/15, Lunokhod 1/2 and Chandrayaan-3 all are, at
    six independently published coordinates (see section 10.1).
    (V) 'As of 2009, the distance to the Moon can be measured with
    millimeter precision' -- and 'Modern Lunar Laser Ranging data can be
    fit with a 1 cm weighted rms residual.'
    (V) Replication: French scientists at the Cote d'Azur Observatory
    reproduced the Lunokhod 1 ranging in May 2013.
    NOTE ON CONSISTENCY WITH SECTION 10.1: that block's ~1-5 photon
    applies to the 1970s-90s ruby-era return from the Apollo 11 array.
    Modern stations report different numbers from the same arrays.
    (V) 'We got about 2,000 photons from Lunokhod 1 on our first try.'
    The two figures are not in conflict; they are different instruments:
    ~1e3-1e4 photons per *shot* with a 3.5 m telescope, adaptive optics
    and a ~100 ps pulse is a different epoch from ~1-5 photons with a
    3.1 m telescope and a 6.5 km beam.  The direction of the change is
    itself evidence that the return is beam-divergence-limited, which is
    what a discrete array predicts and a surface does not.

B5. A vividness check on the source's own analogy (recomputed).
    (V) 'equivalent in accuracy to determining the distance between Los
    Angeles and New York to within the width of a human hair.'
    LA-NY / hair = 7.88e+10 : 1  for a hair of 50.0 um.
    LA-NY / hair = 6.57e+10 : 1  for a hair of 60.0 um.
    LA-NY / hair = 5.63e+10 : 1  for a hair of 70.0 um.

