BLOCK C -- Claim (c2): the Roswell/Mogul trajectory, from verified
           gazetteer coordinates. This is the item pass 4 left open.
==============================================================================
    (V) Alamogordo, NM  32.856111  -105.974444   [launch site: 'Alamogordo AAF']
    (V) Corona, NM      34.247778  -105.596944   [find vicinity]
    (V) Roswell, NM     33.3942  -104.5228   [Roswell Army Air Field]

    Great-circle (WGS-84 mean radius) Alamogordo -> Corona: 158.7 km,
    initial bearing 12.6 deg (compass 'NE', i.e. +12.6 deg E of N).
    Alamogordo -> Roswell (RAAF, the reporting base): 147.8 km.
    (V) VERIFIED verbatim, 'Roswell incident': 'On June 4, researchers
    at Alamogordo Army Air Field in New Mexico launched a long train of
    these balloons; they lost contact with the balloons and balloon-
    borne equipment within 17 miles (27 km) of the ranch managed by
    W. W. "Mac" Brazel near Corona, New Mexico, where a balloon array
    subsequently crashed.'

    CAVEAT, stated plainly: Corona is the nearest gazetted town to the
    Brazel ranch, which lies a few km further NE of it, so 159 km is a
    LOWER BOUND on the required launch-to-find displacement.  I take
    +10% as a rounding allowance and scan 130-200 km below.

    A constant-altitude balloon CANNOT AIM: its track is the passive
    integral of the wind at its altitude.  So the Mogul hypothesis
    makes a checkable prediction, and the required mean drift speed is
    simply (displacement)/(time aloft):

      time aloft      required mean drift speed (km/h / m/s)
      -------------------------------------------------------
          1 h          158.7 km/h =   44.1 m/s
          3 h           52.9 km/h =   14.7 m/s
          6 h           26.4 km/h =    7.3 m/s
         12 h           13.2 km/h =    3.7 m/s
         18 h            8.8 km/h =    2.4 m/s
         24 h            6.6 km/h =    1.8 m/s
         36 h            4.4 km/h =    1.2 m/s
         48 h            3.3 km/h =    0.9 m/s
         72 h            2.2 km/h =    0.6 m/s
        168 h            0.9 km/h =    0.3 m/s
        720 h            0.2 km/h =    0.1 m/s

    READ THIS AS: the open item from pass 4 is now a stated, checkable
    number rather than a hand-wave.  If Flight 4 was aloft for T hours,
    its mean wind between ~9 and ~15 km over southern New Mexico must
    have been (158.7/T) km/h from the SW-SSW, T in hours.  To settle it
    it, one needs the June 1947 upper-air wind field (radiosonde archive
    or a long-period reanalysis), which this session did NOT retrieve.

    Debris-field geometry: the several-acres scatter.
    (V) 'tinfoil, rubber, tape, and thin wooden beams scattered across
    several acres of the ranch.'
      3 acres =   12141 m^2 -> equivalent square 110 m on a side.
      4 acres =   16187 m^2 -> equivalent square 127 m on a side.
      5 acres =   20234 m^2 -> equivalent square 142 m on a side.
    A compact impact site of a ~10 m craft would concentrate nearly all
    recoverable mass into one location, with fragments thrown O(10 m).
    A field 100-140 m across with 'no engine or metal parts' (the 9 July
    1947 Roswell Daily Record, verified in source 4) is what a long
    balloon train shredding and dropping material over its length does.
    Because the source gives only the AREA, not the shape or the
    length/width ratio, this is an argument of direction, not a
    precision measurement.

    The 8 July 1947 FBI telex, verbatim (V): 'The disc is hexagonal in
    shape and was suspended from a balloon by cable, which balloon was
    approximately twenty feet (6.1 m) in diameter.'
    A 6.1 m envelope, treated as a superpressure sphere at 9-15 km:
      altitude  9 km (ISA, A): T = 229.6 K, p = 30742 Pa, rho_air = 0.466 kg/m^3
        envelope volume 118.8 m^3, gross lift 468 N = 47.8 kg
        helium mass 7.7 kg -> net payload ~40.1 kg before envelope mass.
      altitude 12 km (ISA, A): T = 216.7 K, p = 19330 Pa, rho_air = 0.311 kg/m^3
        envelope volume 118.8 m^3, gross lift 312 N = 31.8 kg
        helium mass 5.1 kg -> net payload ~26.7 kg before envelope mass.
      altitude 15 km (ISA, A): T = 216.7 K, p = 12044 Pa, rho_air = 0.194 kg/m^3
        envelope volume 118.8 m^3, gross lift 195 N = 19.8 kg
        helium mass 3.2 kg -> net payload ~16.7 kg before envelope mass.
    A Mogul train (balloons + sonobuoys + radios + cable) is a
    payload of order that size; a crewed interstellar vehicle with
    'bodies' is not.  (A) pressures/masses use the standard atmosphere.
    This is a scale check -- the load-bearing datum remains that the
    USAF's report identified the specific train and the 27 km miss.

    Base rate (V): 'By 1947, the United States had launched THOUSANDS
    of top-secret Project Mogul balloons.'  Thousands of long balloon
    trains were aloft over New Mexico in 1947; debris falls were
    routine, and only this one became a legend.  The Blue Book record
    (V) independently notes that 'a number of the reports could be
    explained by flights of the formerly secret reconnaissance planes
    U-2 and A-12' -- i.e. same lesson, same sky, later decade.

