BLOCK D -- Claim (d): base rates from Project Blue Book, and two
           narrative-internal quantities
==============================================================================

D1. Project Blue Book (V, 'Project Blue Book'): March 1952 - 17 Dec
    1969, HQ Wright-Patterson AFB.
    (V) 'By the time Project Blue Book ended, it had collected 12,618
    UFO reports, and concluded that most of them were misidentifications
    of natural phenomena (clouds, stars, etc.) or conventional aircraft.'
    (V) '701 reports were classified as unexplained, even after stringent
    analysis.'
    (V) 'By the time of the hearing, Blue Book had identified and
    explained 95% of the reported UFO sightings.'

    computed: identified = 94.44% ; unexplained = 5.56%
    (V) the two official conclusions, verbatim:
      'There was no evidence submitted to or discovered by the Air
       Force that sightings categorized as "unidentified" represented
       technological developments or principles beyond the range of
       present-day scientific knowledge.'
      'There was no evidence indicating that sightings categorized as
       "unidentified" were extraterrestrial vehicles.'
    (V) the Condon Report 'concluded that the study of UFOs was unlikely
    to yield major scientific discoveries'.
    SO: over 17 years, 12,618 reports, 701 left open -- and 0 of the 701
    produced physical, photographic, radar or biological evidence.
    That is the quantitatively stated absence for claim (d).

D2. Two NARRATIVE-INTERNAL quantities (new; both from 'Alien
    abduction', (V)):
    (i) Hopkins' own count of 'alien mistakes': 'Hopkins has estimated
    that these "errors" accompany 4-5 percent of abduction reports'
    -- the 'cosmic application of Murphy's Law': failing to return the
    experiencer to the same spot, or putting clothes on backwards.
    An entity with interstellar transport makes mundane handling errors
    in 1 in 20-25 reports.  That ratio is a property of fiction, not of
    engineering.
    (ii) Mack 'interviewed over 800 people' -- i.e. the proponent with
    the largest clinical sample still produced no physical artefact.
    (V) the Lancet's Niall Boyce on Mack: 'a well-meaning man uncritically
    elaborating on tales of alien abduction, and potentially both
    cementing and constructing false memories'.

D3. Two further artifacts of the abduction claim, both (V) and both
    disconfirmed rather than merely unsupported:
    - the 2017 'Kodachrome slides' of a dead alien (Jaime Maussan):
      'the slides were in fact of a mummified Native American child
      discovered in 1896 and which had been on display at the Chapin
      Mesa Archeological Museum in Mesa Verde, Colorado, for many
      decades.'
    - a 2020-declassified c.1951 incident: two Roswell personnel in
      'poorly fitting radioactive suits, complete with oxygen masks,
      while retrieving a weather balloon after an atomic test', met a
      woman who fainted; the historian notes they could have appeared
      'to someone unaccustomed to then-modern gear, to be alien.'
    Both are 'disconfirming' evidence for the artefacts-as-offered, not
    'absent' evidence.

